{
  "slug": "serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree",
  "title": "Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "breadth-first tree construction followed by a preorder serialization",
  "statement": "A binary tree is described by a level-order array `level`. The tree is built in this way: `level[0]` is the value of the root, and a value of `0` there means the tree is empty. The nodes are then processed in the order in which they were created, starting with the root. When a node is processed, it takes the next two unused values of `level` as the value of its left child slot and then the value of its right child slot; a slot whose value is `0` creates no node and is therefore never processed itself, a used slot is never reused, and when `level` runs out of values the corresponding child simply does not exist. Values left over after the last created node was processed belong to no node and are ignored. Every real node has a value of at least `1`.\n\nSerialize the tree that `level` describes into a string: walk it in preorder (the node, then its left subtree, then its right subtree), write the node values separated by commas, and write `#` at every empty child position. Each real node therefore contributes exactly three comma separated tokens: its own value, then its left position, then its right position. Return that string; the serialization of the empty tree is the single token `#`.\n\nFor example `level = [1, 2, 3, 0, 0, 4]` describes a tree whose root is `1` with left child `2` and right child `3`, and `4` as the left child of `3`; its serialization is `1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,#`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= level.length <= 200` and `0 <= level[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "level = [0]",
      "output": "\"#\"",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "level = [4889, 8317, 9977, 3258]",
      "output": "\"4889,8317,3258,#,#,#,9977,#,#\"",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(level: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Created nodes take their array slots strictly in creation order, two slots per node, so a simple breadth-first simulation with one pointer into the array reproduces the tree exactly.",
    "A slot is consumed even when its value is 0; the 0 only means the child does not exist, and its own two slots are never handed out.",
    "The serialization is a plain preorder walk in which a missing child prints #, so every real node always produces exactly three tokens no matter how the tree is shaped."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The rule that every created node consumes exactly two consecutive array values makes the tree reachable by one breadth-first simulation. The optimal solution keeps the created nodes in a queue in creation order and a pointer into `level`; for each node it reads the value at the pointer for the left child and the value after it for the right child, creating a node only when the value is not `0` and the pointer is still inside the array, and advancing the pointer twice either way. Nodes are appended to the same array that is being iterated, so the queue costs nothing extra. A preorder walk of the finished tree then writes the values and a `#` for every missing child. The brute force skips the queue and works out a node's position from the array alone: the creation index of a node is the number of non-zero values from the start of `level` up to and including its own slot, and that index `k` tells you its children live in slots `2*k + 1` and `2*k + 2`. Recomputing that count by rescanning the array for every node visited is what makes the reference solution quadratic.",
    "why_optimal": "Every value of `level` is examined once and each created node is visited once, so linear time is the input size bound; there can be at most `(n+1)/2` real nodes because each one consumes two array values under the two-slot rule, and the output string already has three tokens per node, so writing the answer is linear in n as well. The quadratic reference arises only from recomputing the creation index, which the queue makes unnecessary.",
    "pitfalls": "Treating `0` as a node value, or as an ordinary child that still gets processed. Forgetting that a slot is consumed even when its value is `0`, which shifts every node after it and silently changes the tree. Using the array index of a node as if it were its creation index: they differ as soon as one hole appears, so the child slots must be derived from the creation order. Forgetting the two `#` positions for a node's empty children, which breaks the three tokens per node contract. Ignoring the trailing array values that no node ever consumed. Returning an empty string instead of `#` for the empty tree, which is the `level[0] == 0` case. Using recursion deep enough to matter on a 200 element skewed input."
  },
